  nonce: c4c1  # c4c1
  data:  # c4c1
    kind: invariant  # c4c1
    title: "--backend tree-sitter does not disable the rust-analyzer backend when HYPERGUMBO_RUST_ANALYZER=1: the advertised opt-out is inert and the run executes the repo's build.rs anyway"  # c4c1
    status: violated  # c4c1
    priority: 1  # c4c1
    parent:  # c4c1
    tags:  # c4c1
    - rust  # c4c1
    - security  # c4c1
    - multi_fidelity  # c4c1
    - adr_0045  # c4c1
    isbefore: []  # c4c1
    duplicate_of: []  # c4c1
    not_duplicate_of: []  # c4c1
    pr_ref:  # c4c1
    description: "REPRODUCED END-TO-END 2026-08-23 on dev cd8b98bbcd, authored fixture\n~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/rust_fidelity_probe_08232026/ (no build.rs, no deps):\n\n  XDG_CACHE_HOME=/tmp/hgcache-armC HYPERGUMBO_RUST_ANALYZER=1 \\\n    hypergumbo verify-claims <fixture> --claims <claims> --backend tree-sitter\n\n  -> \"UserWarning: rust-analyzer backend ACTIVE for this run\"\n  -> 10 nodes carrying origin=['scip'] out of 26\n\nSo --backend tree-sitter did NOT disable the backend. Byte-identical node\npopulation to the arm that asked for rust-analyzer explicitly.\n\nWHY THIS IS SECURITY-RELEVANT AND NOT A TIDINESS BUG. The tool's own warning\ntext advertises this exact escape hatch -- check_rust_analyzer_disclosure\nprints \"Disable per run: --backend tree-sitter\" -- and the same warning\ndiscloses that indexing \"IS RUNNING this project's build scripts (build.rs)\nand proc macros as you\". A user who has HYPERGUMBO_RUST_ANALYZER=1 set (which\nis the ONLY durable opt-in hypergumbo offers today -- see WI-sobig) and who\ndeliberately opts OUT for one repository they do not trust WILL EXECUTE THAT\nREPOSITORY'S build.rs ANYWAY, having been told by the tool that they turned it\noff. The failure direction is toward arbitrary code execution, and it bites\nprecisely the users who followed the only persistence route available.\n\nMECHANISM, read in code. cli.py's argv preprocessing (the WI-vozof block,\ncli.py:11305-11322) handles --backend in any position:\n\n    if choice == \"rust-analyzer\":\n        _ensure_rust_analyzer_integration_or_exit()\n        _ensure_rust_analyzer_binary_or_exit()\n        os.environ[\"HYPERGUMBO_RUST_ANALYZER\"] = \"1\"\n    argv = argv[:idx] + argv[idx + 2:]\n\nThe rust-analyzer arm sets the env var; there is NO tree-sitter arm. The flag\nis stripped from argv either way, so the gate never receives backend_flag at\nall in production -- it reads only the env var, which --backend tree-sitter\nnever touches. gate.should_use_rust_analyzer_backend is NOT at fault: it takes\nbackend_flag and environ as separate injectable parameters and would honour a\nflag if one reached it. The conflation is entirely in the CLI layer, which\nlaunders the flag INTO the env var and thereby destroys the distinction the\nprecedence order needs.\n\nRELATION TO ADR-0045 (merged cd8b98bbcd). This is ruling 4 -- \"Precedence, and\nstop laundering the flag through the environment: CLI flag > environment\nvariable > project config > user config > built-in default\" -- with a live\nrepro attached. The ADR named the laundering as an obstacle to ADDING config\ntiers; this item records that it is ALREADY producing a wrong answer with only\ntwo tiers present, so the fix is owed independently of the config work.\n\nTHE FIX SHAPE, per ADR-0045 ruling 4: one resolver that owns the decision,\nconsulted by both the CLI and the gate, rather than a second home for the fact.\n--backend tree-sitter must resolve to an EXPLICIT off that outranks the env\nvar, not to a silent no-op.\n\nNOT MEASURED: whether any other flag in the WI-vozof preprocessing block has\nthe same missing-negative-arm shape; whether --backend tree-sitter is\nsimilarly inert against a future config tier (it cannot be tested until one\nexists)."  # c4c1
    fields:  # c4c1
      statement: "A CLI flag must outrank the environment variable that expresses the same choice: --backend tree-sitter disables the SCIP backend regardless of HYPERGUMBO_RUST_ANALYZER, and the disable path the tool advertises in its own warning must actually disable."  # c4c1
      root_cause: "cli.py's WI-vozof argv preprocessing (cli.py:11305-11322) translates --backend into os.environ[HYPERGUMBO_RUST_ANALYZER] but implements only the rust-analyzer arm; the tree-sitter arm strips the flag and sets nothing. Because the flag is stripped, gate.should_use_rust_analyzer_backend never receives backend_flag in production and reads only the env var, which the opt-out never clears."  # c4c1
- op: discuss  # d5d1
  at: '2026-08-23T18:28:44Z'  # d5d1
  by: agent  # d5d1
  actor: jgstern_agent  # d5d1
  clock: 2  # d5d1
  nonce: d5d1  # d5d1
  message: "ADJACENT DEFECT FOUND WHILE FIXING THIS ONE, deliberately NOT folded into the\nsame PR, and recorded here so it is not lost.\n\n*** --backend <unrecognised-value> IS SILENTLY SWALLOWED. *** The argparse\ndeclaration constrains the flag (choices=[\"tree-sitter\", \"rust-analyzer\"]),\nbut cli.py's argv preprocessing strips --backend and its argument from argv\nBEFORE argparse runs, and it strips unconditionally -- the choice is inspected\nto decide what to record, not to decide whether to consume. So the choices=\nconstraint never sees the global flag at all, and `hypergumbo survey .\n--backend nonsense` runs normally with the flag discarded rather than erroring.\n\nHOW IT WAS FOUND, which is worth recording because it is a case of the test\nbeing wrong rather than the code. Writing the precedence tests for this item I\nasserted that `--backend nonsense` would raise SystemExit via argparse. It did\nnot. I checked the code before changing the assertion, and the code is right on\nthe point the test was actually about -- an unrecognised choice must be\nNO-OPINION at the flag tier, so that a typo cannot silently override a lower\ntier's explicit opt-in in either direction. The test now asserts that property\n(test_an_unrecognised_backend_leaves_the_opt_in_alone) and names the swallowing\nin its docstring as separate and unfixed.\n\nWHY NOT FIXED HERE. It is a usability defect, not a precedence one, and the fix\nhas a real design question attached that this PR should not answer by default:\nwhether to reject unknown values at preprocessing time (a new error path, and\nthe flag is accepted in any argv position which makes \"did the user mean this\nas a value?\" less obvious than it looks), or to stop stripping and let argparse\nown the rejection (bigger change -- the stripping exists precisely because the\nflag must work before subcommand dispatch, per WI-vozof).\n\nSEVERITY, honestly: much lower than this item's. The failure is a flag that\ndoes nothing rather than a flag that does the opposite of what it says, and it\ncannot enable the backend -- an unrecognised value writes nothing, which the\nnew test pins. Worth a separate low-priority item when someone touches this\nblock again."  # d5d1
